WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:03.410
The African elephant population

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:07.370
has been decimated by poaching
over the past 10 years.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.890
If it continues, this iconic animal

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:11.800
could be extinct within decades.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.300
Elephants are killed for their ivory.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.410
A single tusk can fetch upwards of $4,000

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.000
on the black market.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.780
I think the realization
has finally hit home

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.280
that if we don't stop this trade,

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.450
we are in big trouble.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:28.930
The slaughter is disastrous,

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.480
not just for elephants,
but for the stability

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.500
of African nations.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.840
Almost every recent African conflict

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.010
has been fueled by money from ivory.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.650
But, it can be stopped.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:54.100
In 1989, in an attempt to save
the last African elephants,

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.260
the species was awarded the highest level

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.220
of protection under the
Convention on International Trade

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.480
in Endangered Species, or CITES.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:07.080
This meant all trade in
elephant parts was prohibited.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.576
It was probably the most important

00:01:09.576 --> 00:01:12.630
international legislation
that has ever been done

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.850
for any wildlife because
it literally stopped

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.150
poaching across the continent overnight.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.890
The ban was so successful
that elephants were starting

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.700
to really increase in numbers.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.060
But, the CITES legislation
included a clause.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.330
It stated that countries

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.221
with well-managed elephant populations

00:01:29.221 --> 00:01:31.240
could downgrade their elephants

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.950
to a lower level of protection.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.760
This allowed regulated trade and sales

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:38.570
of existing ivory stockpiles.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.770
That was the most devastating change

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.870
that could possibly have been made

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:48.070
because it opened the
doors for dual listing

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:49.980
which has created all the problems

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.640
that we are seeing right now.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:54.320
One of the biggest sales

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.350
of stockpiled ivory was in 2008,

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.710
when 102 tons was sold off.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.650
Initially, it was seen as
good news for the elephants

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:07.830
because it raised $15 million
for local African communities,

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.550
much of which was spent
on elephant conservation.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.140
But, during the same period,
undercover investigators

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.430
reported a spike in ivory
sales on the black market.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.630
Operating for over 30 years,

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:28.140
the Environmental
Investigation Agency, or EIA,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.980
has used undercover techniques to try

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.330
to penetrate wildlife trafficking gangs.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:37.770
They found the legal sell-off in 2008

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.300
revived the ivory market.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.000
From our investigations,
we're actually engaged

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:42.833
with some of the traders.

00:02:42.833 --> 00:02:44.800
We get the impression
that there was a feeling

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.720
when the legal sale was allowed that,

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:47.720
that business was back.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.370
It was okay to trade ivory again.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.110
The legal sell-off provided

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.750
the perfect cover for the illegal trade

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.220
because it's hard to tell
if an individual tusk

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:00.430
is from a legal stockpile,
or has been poached.

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:04.220
Around 70% of the
world's ivory is destined

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:07.700
for China where it's
revered a symbol of wealth.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.790
Intricate carvings can fetch hundreds

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.140
of thousands of dollars.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:16.353
EIA investigators found that around 90%

00:03:16.353 --> 00:03:19.440
of so-called legal ivory on sale in China

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:20.740
was from the black market.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.070
But, once they make a seizure,

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.430
the EIA need evidence that the tusks

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.090
have been illegally poached.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.780
Dr. Sam Wasser has developed a novel way

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:34.810
to prove where ivory tusks have come from

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:36.980
using elephant DNA.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:38.860
My laboratory was one of the first

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.710
to develop ways to get
DNA out of elephant dung

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:45.300
from elephants across their
entire range over Africa.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:49.530
The dung samples enabled Dr. Wasser

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:53.320
to map the location of
individual elephant herds.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:57.010
DNA samples from seized
tusks can be cross-referenced

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:00.410
with Dr. Wasser's database
to see if there's a match.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.950
We were shocked to find
that over the last decade,

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:09.360
virtually 100% of those
large seizures came

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.440
from just two places.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.670
78% of it was Savannah ivory starting

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.270
from northern Mozambique
up through Tanzania

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:19.070
into the southern border of Kenya.

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:23.130
And you could see over time
how it slowly crept North.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.820
The other major hotspot
was from a place called

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.270
the Tridom, and that was
all forest elephants.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.630
Using radiocarbon dating,

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.470
Dr. Wasser and his team are able

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.730
to determine the age of the tusks.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:40.060
So, we took samples from
all of the large seizures

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.340
that we had, and we did isotope analysis

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.240
on each of those seizures and we found

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.390
that virtually all that
ivory was new ivory.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:51.500
This research helps conservationists

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.520
know where to focus their efforts.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.450
It also provides valuable evidence

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.540
when prosecuting ivory traffickers.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.110
Stamping out the illegal trade

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.890
is not just a conservation issue.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.780
The trade is run by
organized criminal networks

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:12.110
and the money funds militant
and terrorist groups.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.930
The Lord's Resistance Army in Uganda,

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:19.560
Boko Haram in Nigeria
and Al-Shabaab in Somali

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.110
have all been funded by money from ivory.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:27.810
But, time could be up
on the illegal trade.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:29.960
Some African countries are destroying

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:32.950
their stockpiles to prevent
them from ever being sold.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:38.910
Kenya burned over 100 tons of ivory,

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:42.270
and that whole cascade of
events caused the price

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:47.270
of ivory to drop from
about $2,000 a kilo in 2013

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.270
to now around eight to $700 a kilo.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:55.060
In July 2016, President Obama's

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.300
near total ban on the buying and selling

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.040
of ivory in America came into effect.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:06.520
In December 2017, China, the
world's biggest ivory market,

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.370
shut down its domestic trade.

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:11.470
Hong Kong soon followed suit.

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:16.420
Britain, the world's biggest
exporter of legal ivory,

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:18.660
is consulting on a total ban,

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.070
but has yet to implement it.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.950
Closing the last legal markets
will stifle demand for ivory,

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:29.190
and remove the cover the
illegal trade relies upon.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.770
If we can shut the ivory
markets down globally

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:37.400
and domestically so that
we eliminate the demand,

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.600
then we have hope to save the elephant.

